WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.894
Last time, one line gave us a kitchen: four cooks, taking tickets from one rail.

2
00:00:07.894 --> 00:00:09.582
Then comes a busy evening.

3
00:00:09.582 --> 00:00:16.139
Orders arrive faster than four cooks can cook them, and the rail grows by a few tickets every second.

4
00:00:16.367 --> 00:00:21.980
So someone rebuilds the pool with a maximum of 64 threads, and keeps the same rail.

5
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.198
The code compiles.

6
00:00:23.198 --> 00:00:25.024
The setting is right there.

7
00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:27.188
And not one extra cook turns up.

8
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.518
So here's a question many of us are too shy to ask.

9
00:00:30.518 --> 00:00:33.515
What is a maximum for, if it never brings anyone?

10
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.658
To find out, let's visit a kitchen with a thrifty manager.

11
00:00:40.658 --> 00:00:42.361
It has two permanent cooks.

12
00:00:42.361 --> 00:00:44.506
Its rail has room for two tickets.

13
00:00:44.506 --> 00:00:49.804
When the rail is full, the manager may phone for temporary cooks, up to two of them.

14
00:00:49.804 --> 00:00:56.489
And when every cook is busy and the rail is full, a door policy decides what happens to the next customer.

15
00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:02.104
ThreadPoolExecutor, the class behind that one line, has a setting for each part.

16
00:01:02.104 --> 00:01:05.143
The core pool size is the permanent cooks: 2.

17
00:01:05.143 --> 00:01:09.534
The maximum pool size counts permanent and temporary together: 4.

18
00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:13.992
The work queue is the rail, an ArrayBlockingQueue with room for 2.

19
00:01:13.992 --> 00:01:17.032
And the rejection handler is the door policy.

20
00:01:17.032 --> 00:01:19.801
The default, AbortPolicy, simply refuses.

21
00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:25.144
Now seven orders arrive at once, and each one takes 500 milliseconds to cook.

22
00:01:25.144 --> 00:01:27.135
Most of us expect the obvious.

23
00:01:27.135 --> 00:01:32.511
Four cooks start right away, two orders wait on the rail, and one is turned away.

24
00:01:32.511 --> 00:01:33.905
Is that what happens?

25
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:39.545
Let's follow the rules, one order at a time.

26
00:01:39.545 --> 00:01:44.912
Order 1: there are fewer cooks than the core size, so it gets a new permanent cook.

27
00:01:44.912 --> 00:01:45.817
That's rule 1.

28
00:01:45.817 --> 00:01:47.628
Order 2 gets the second one.

29
00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:52.529
Order 3: we're at the core size, so the manager clips it to the rail.

30
00:01:52.529 --> 00:01:53.482
That's rule 2.

31
00:01:53.482 --> 00:01:56.408
Order 4 joins it, and now the rail is full.

32
00:01:56.633 --> 00:01:58.530
Order 5 finds the rail full.

33
00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:03.612
Only now does the manager pick up the phone, and a temporary cook takes it.

34
00:02:03.612 --> 00:02:04.560
That's rule 3.

35
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.439
Order 6 gets the second temp, and the kitchen is at its maximum of four.

36
00:02:09.667 --> 00:02:13.024
Order 7 finds every cook busy and the rail full.

37
00:02:13.024 --> 00:02:18.619
That's rule 4: the door policy turns it away, with a RejectedExecutionException.

38
00:02:18.833 --> 00:02:20.836
So the obvious guess was wrong.

39
00:02:20.836 --> 00:02:23.743
The pool doesn't grow first and queue second.

40
00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.940
It queues first, and grows second.

41
00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.942
And that's the thrifty manager.

42
00:02:27.942 --> 00:02:30.204
A ticket on the rail costs nothing.

43
00:02:30.204 --> 00:02:32.077
A temporary cook costs wages.

44
00:02:32.077 --> 00:02:35.243
So he phones only when the rail has no room left.

45
00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:36.805
One more surprise.

46
00:02:36.805 --> 00:02:43.496
Orders 1, 2, 5 and 6 come out at about 500 milliseconds, and orders 3 and 4 only at 1,000.

47
00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:50.484
A new cook takes the ticket that made the manager call him, not the oldest ticket on the rail.

48
00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.979
Here the restaurant picture breaks.

49
00:02:52.979 --> 00:02:58.709
A real kitchen would cook the oldest ticket first, or hear about it from tables 3 and 4.

50
00:02:58.709 --> 00:03:00.597
A pool makes no such promise.

51
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.540
Now back to our 64 cooks who never came.

52
00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:09.567
newFixedThreadPool builds this same class, with a LinkedBlockingQueue that has no limit.

53
00:03:09.567 --> 00:03:10.868
A rail with no end.

54
00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.973
So rule 2 always succeeds.

55
00:03:12.973 --> 00:03:16.286
Rule 3 never runs, and no temp is ever called.

56
00:03:16.286 --> 00:03:20.319
Rule 4 never runs either, so nobody is ever turned away.

57
00:03:20.319 --> 00:03:25.505
The maximum of 64 is dead code: it compiles, and it never does anything.

58
00:03:25.733 --> 00:03:32.885
Run the same seven orders with that queue, and the pool stays at two threads, with five tickets on the rail.

59
00:03:32.885 --> 00:03:38.248
All seven finish after about 2,000 milliseconds: four rounds of 500 on two cooks.

60
00:03:38.467 --> 00:03:39.618
That looks safe.

61
00:03:39.618 --> 00:03:40.266
It isn't.

62
00:03:40.266 --> 00:03:45.017
Say orders arrive at 400 a second, and the kitchen can finish 300.

63
00:03:45.017 --> 00:03:47.537
The rail grows by 100 every second.

64
00:03:47.537 --> 00:03:56.463
After ten minutes, 60,000 tickets are waiting, and a new order waits more than three minutes before a cook even looks at it.

65
00:03:56.667 --> 00:04:01.830
The customer left long ago, and the kitchen is busy cooking dinners nobody will eat.

66
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:07.240
And nothing complains until the heap, the memory where Java keeps its objects, runs out.

67
00:04:07.467 --> 00:04:10.036
newCachedThreadPool fails the other way.

68
00:04:10.036 --> 00:04:14.982
Its rail has no room at all, so under a burst, every order gets a new thread.

69
00:04:14.982 --> 00:04:18.836
That's last lesson's manager, hiring a cook for every salad.

70
00:04:19.067 --> 00:04:24.326
The shape that pushes back is a rail with a limit, and a maximum that isn't endless.

71
00:04:24.326 --> 00:04:30.024
Then rule 4 can fire, and overload becomes something you can see, and choose how to handle.

72
00:04:30.233 --> 00:04:32.847
Here's our kitchen in Java and Kotlin.

73
00:04:32.847 --> 00:04:40.345
Core 2, max 4, and a keep-alive of 60 seconds, which is how long an idle temp may stay before he's sent home.

74
00:04:40.345 --> 00:04:43.096
Then the rail of 2, and the door policy.

75
00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:44.962
So, what is a maximum for?

76
00:04:44.962 --> 00:04:47.390
It's the relief valve for a full rail.

77
00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.946
The pool queues first, and grows second.

78
00:04:49.946 --> 00:04:54.356
Give the queue a limit, or the maximum is just a number in your code.

79
00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:55.400
Quick check.

80
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.385
Core 2, max 4, and a rail with room for 10.

81
00:04:58.385 --> 00:05:01.508
Eight orders arrive, and each takes a minute.

82
00:05:01.508 --> 00:05:03.383
How many cooks are cooking?

83
00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:06.650
Two.

84
00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:12.247
The other six wait on the rail, which never fills, so the manager never phones.
